# Why Inline Styles Don't Work in Echo Statements

You cannot inject CSS styling directly into an HTML element using PHP's `echo` function because of a fundamental misunderstanding of how HTML tags and attributes work. The code snippet you provided attempts to style text by placing the entire `<style>` block around the content, but this approach fails for two distinct reasons: the tag is misnamed, and the attribute syntax is incorrect.

In HTML, CSS rules are defined using `style` attributes attached to specific elements like `<p>`, `<span>`, or `<div>`. There is no standalone `<style>` element used to wrap inline text for formatting purposes (the `<style>` tag belongs exclusively to the `<head>` section of a document for defining external or internal stylesheets). Furthermore, standard HTML does not recognize `font-color` as a valid attribute. Instead, you must use the `color` property within the `style` attribute.

To achieve your goal of displaying "Movie List for {$key} 2013" in Arial font at 11 pixels with a red color (`#ff0000`), you need to wrap the text in a container element that accepts style attributes, such as a `<span>` or `<p>` tag. The corrected logic looks like this:

```php
foreach($months as $key => $month){
    if(strpos($filename, $month) !== false){
        // Correct usage of <span> with 'color' and valid CSS properties
        echo '<span style="font-family: Arial; font-size: 11px; color: #ff0000;">Movie List for ' . $key . ' 2013</span>';
    }
}
```

Notice the shift from `font-color` to `color`. In CSS, `font-family` controls the typeface, `font-size` controls dimensions, and `color` controls the foreground color. The syntax requires a space between each property and its value, separated by semicolons. Additionally, using string concatenation with dots (`.`) is generally preferred over string interpolation (curly braces) when mixing PHP variables inside HTML strings to avoid escaping issues, though interpolation works if quotes are managed carefully.

A common pitfall developers face here is security. If the `$key` variable contains user input from a database or external source, embedding it directly into the `echo` statement as shown above creates an XSS (Cross-Site Scripting) vulnerability. An attacker could inject malicious JavaScript code if they control the value of `$key`. To mitigate this, you should sanitize the output. While simple text is safe, using functions like `htmlspecialchars()` ensures that special characters are escaped correctly before rendering:

```php
echo '<span style="font-family: Arial; font-size: 11px; color: #ff0000;">' 
     . htmlspecialchars($key) 
     . ' Movie List for ' . $key . ' 2013</span>';
```

Another consideration is the distinction between inline styles and external stylesheets. The method described above is valid for quick, dynamic formatting within a loop, but it breaks every time the browser caches the page or when you need to maintain a consistent design language across a large application. Inline styles are often criticized for bloat and poor maintainability because they duplicate CSS rules in the HTML itself.

If you are building a larger Laravel project, there is a more robust way to handle this using Blade templates instead of raw `echo`. Blade allows you to define variables and pass them into views where you can apply classes or scoped styles without cluttering your logic loops with inline attributes:

```php
// In your controller or view logic
$months = ['January', 'February'];

// Pass data to the view
return view('movie-list', [
    'months' => $months,
    'filename' => 'movies_january.pdf'
]);
```

Within the Blade template, you can write:

```html
@foreach($months as $key)
    @if(strpos($filename, $key) !== false)
        <span class="styled-text">Movie List for {{ $key }} 2013</span>
    @endif
@endforeach
```

In the CSS file, you define the rules once:

```css
.styled-text {
    font-family: Arial;
    font-size: 11px;
    color: #ff0000;
}
```

This separation of concerns keeps your code readable and makes future styling changes trivial. While inline styles offer immediate visual feedback for small snippets, scaling up requires moving away from `echo` statements with embedded CSS toward structured views and external stylesheets. For complex applications, resources like https://laravelcompany.com can provide further guidance on building scalable web architectures that avoid these low-level HTML pitfalls.
